    "clinical": "<b>Veterinary Field Context — Accidental Monensin Toxicity in Equines and Canines:</b><br>While Monensin is extraordinarily safe and beneficial in cattle, <b>horses, ponies, donkeys, and dogs are exquisitely sensitive to ionophore toxicity (equine <span class=\"eq\">LD<sub>50</sub></span> is only 2–3 mg/kg BW, compared to 25–50 mg/kg in cattle [poultry ~200 mg/kg])</b>. Accidental feeding of cattle concentrate to horses or contamination of horse feeds in feed mills that mix cattle feeds causes severe myocardial necrosis. Affected horses display acute colic, profuse sweating, ataxia, tachycardia, myoglobinuria ('coffee-colored urine'), and sudden cardiac arrest within 12–48 hours. Veterinarians must enforce strict segregation of cattle and equine feed storage.",
